      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{24}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le 24.$ Find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\le y)=0.95.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $24$ | $\\,42.980\\,$ | $\\,36.415\\,$ | $\\,33.196\\,$ | $\\,31.132\\,$ | $\\,29.553\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,44.314\\,$ | $\\,37.652\\,$ | $\\,34.382\\,$ | $\\,32.282\\,$ | $\\,30.675\\,$ |\n| $26$ | $\\,45.642\\,$ | $\\,38.885\\,$ | $\\,35.563\\,$ | $\\,33.429\\,$ | $\\,31.795\\,$ |\n\nThe value of $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\le y)=0.95$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "If ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\n{Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{k}^{2}\n$$\n\nfollows a ${χ}^{2}(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{24}^{2}∼{χ}^{2}(24).\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given probability in the form $P(Y\\ge y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le y) & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ 0.95 & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ P(Y\\ge y) & =0.05\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.05$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $24$ | $\\,42.980\\,$ | $\\,36.415\\,$ | $\\,33.196\\,$ | $\\,31.132\\,$ | $\\,29.553\\,$ |\n| $25$ | $\\,44.314\\,$ | $\\,37.652\\,$ | $\\,34.382\\,$ | $\\,32.282\\,$ | $\\,30.675\\,$ |\n| $26$ | $\\,45.642\\,$ | $\\,38.885\\,$ | $\\,35.563\\,$ | $\\,33.429\\,$ | $\\,31.795\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 36.415)=0.05\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 36.415)=0.95.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=36.415.$",
